          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Accumulate the highest score by accurately bidding and winning tricks over the full hand schedule.',
          gameplay: '1. **Lead**: Left of dealer leads any card. 2. **Following Suit**: Players must follow suit if possible. 3. **The Bloopers**: The Big Blooper is the highest trump, followed by the Little Blooper. If led to a trick, the leader names the suit it represents. 4. **Winning Tricks**: Highest trump or highest card of lead suit wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Board (Bid of 13): A team that bids all tricks accurately in a 13-card hand wins a massive bonus (or the whole game).',
            'Overtricks: Teams are credited for tricks won beyond their bid, but at a lower point value.',
            "Undertricks: A team that fails to reach their combined bid is 'set' and loses significant points.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'In the large-hand deals (13-10), bid conservatively. The penalty for overbidding is much harsher than the reward for overtricks.',
          intermediate: "Use 'Blooper' leads. If you have the Big Blooper, lead it early to smoke out the opponents' trump Aces or the Little Blooper.",
          advanced: "The 'One-Card Hand' (Suicide): When you only have one card, the bidding is pure luck unless you hold a Joker or the Ace of Trumps. Use it as a moment to calibrate your team's overall risk profile.",
          tips: [
            "If your partner bids high (e.g., 5 or 6), only bid your absolute sure tricks. Don't 'double down' and risk putting your team into an unmakeable contract.",
            "Remember: It is 100% legal to win more tricks than you bid. 'Bags' (the overtrick penalty in Spades) do not exist hereâ€”overtricks are pure profit.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
